    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 062300\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n400 PM MST Sun Sep 6 2026\n\n.UPDATE...\nUpdated Aviation\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Aside from some light shower activity lingering across Joshua Tree \nNational Park through this afternoon, mostly dry and quiet weather \nconditions are expected through the first half of this week. \n\n- Thunderstorm chances will start to increase across central and \neastern Arizona by the latter half of the week.\n \n- As high pressure builds over the region, temperatures will heat up \nto above normal levels through the middle of the week. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH TUESDAY/... \nLatest water vapor satellite imagery as well as streamline analysis \nreveal the subtropical high pressure centered over the southern \nPlains while an upper level trough continues to be situated across \nthe Pacific Northwest. This upper level trough will finally begin to \nmigrate east northeastward later today through Monday towards the \nIntermountain West and into south-central Canada by Tuesday while \nweakening in the process. As it does so, it will allow the \nsubtropical ridge to gradually build westward into our neck of the \nwoods during the first half of the upcoming week.\n\nA plume of tropical moisture originating from Hurricane Marie, which \ncontinues to spin well west of the Baja Peninsula Coast, and an \nupper-level jet streak over the Great Basin Region are supporting an \nextensive band of light to locally moderate rainfall which extends \nfrom the southern CA coast east-northeastward towards the Mohave \nDesert Region. The bulk of the activity is expected to remain over \nthese areas through this evening with Joshua Tree National Park \nexpected to be the only area within our CWA to observe some light \nrainfall activity. With this large cluster of rainfall, outflow \nwinds from the northwest are expected to impact much of the lower \ndeserts during the afternoon and evening hours, with peak gusts of \n20-30 mph likely as depicted by the hi-res guidance. Afternoon high \ntemperatures will top out between 102-106 degrees across the south-\ncentral AZ lower deserts with slightly cooler readings between 98-\n105 degrees across the western deserts due to more cloud cover. \n\nConditions will remain quiet during the first half of the week as \nthe overall lack of instability and forcing will hinder storm \nactivity. The bigger story with the building ridge of high pressure \nwill be the rising temperatures. Latest guidance show 500 mb heights \nrising from 590-593dm on Monday to 594-597dm by early Wednesday, \nwhich is near record strength for this time of the year. As a \nresult, temperatures will gradually heat up with the latest NBM now \nshowing lower desert highs reaching the upper 100s to some areas \napproaching 110 degrees by Tuesday and likely be near similar values \nthrough the middle portion of the week. These high temperatures \ncombined with early morning lows in the mid to upper 80s are likely \nto cause HeatRisk levels to reach the major category, and thus if \nthese temperature trends continue, extreme heat products will likely \nbe needed for much of the lower deserts in the subsequent forecast \npackages.\n\n&\n\n.LONG TERM /WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/... \nHeading into the latter half of the workweek, ensemble model \nguidance has come into slightly better agreement on the overall \nsynoptic pattern. The models have come into better agreement on the \nplacement of the features, but still differ on the overall strength \nof the features. Both the GEFS and the EPS show a shortwave trough \nacross the northern CONUS and southern Canada, a broad subtropical \nridge stretching across much of the lower 2/3 of CONUS, along with a \nclosed low off the coast of northern California. The EPS continues \nto be the slightly stronger solution, with a stronger closed low in \nthe Pacific and a slightly stronger subtropical high. This overall \nbetter agreement is reflected with the slightly smaller temperature \nspread in the NBM interquartile range, which has narrowed to less \nthan 5 degrees. The GEFS show the high at peak strength (H5 heights \nof 594-597 dm) on Wednesday before weakening (H5 heights of 591-594 \ndm) by the latter half of the week. The high looks to continue to \nweaken (H5 heights of 588-592 dm) heading into the weekend. \nTherefore, temperatures are forecasted to remain about 5 degrees \nabove normal during the middle of the week before beginning a slight \ncooling trend for the end of the workweek and into the weekend \ncloser to near normal levels. \n\nAs for shower and storm chances, the ensemble members continue to \nshow those chances starting to increase, for at least portions of \nthe CWA, beginning on Wednesday. PWATs look to increase to around \n1.5-1.9\" for the latter half of the week, so moisture should not be \na limiting factor. With the high forecasted to move overhead, rain \nchances will likely be limited to the higher terrain areas across \neastern Arizona on Wednesday and Thursday. Rain chances may extend \ninto the lower deserts of south-central Arizona heading into the \nweekend. However, the current WPC PoPs of 30-40%, for Friday into \nSaturday, seem rather aggressive, especially this far out. WPC's \nSaturday PoPs of 10-30% seem more reasonable.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 2300Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nA period of gusty NW winds this evening will be the greatest weather \nimpact under occasionally thick mid/high cigs. Confidence is \nmoderate that a prolonged period of NW gusts 20-30kt will be common \nthrough the evening before slowly weakening by midnight. This may \ncreate some lofted dust across the region affecting slantwise \nvisibility. Similar to this morning, winds will be delayed making \nthe typical easterly switch, and in some cases like KPHX, may never \ntruly complete the transition. Winds should be noticeably weaker \nMonday afternoon. \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nGusty winds with lofted dust and areas of sfc vsby restriction into \nMVFR category early this evening will be the main weather issue. \nConfidence is good that thick mid/high cigs with virga/sprinkles \nwill persist through overnight with northerly gusts 25-35kt early \nthis evening rapidly weakening by mid evening. However, lofted dust \nand areas with sfc vsby falling into MVFR category will be common \nfor a few hours. Otherwise, a N/NW wind component will be favored \nthrough Monday though periods of nearly calm conditions will become \nmore likely. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nTemperatures will be slightly below normal across SE CA today. \nOtherwise, temperatures will warm to above normal levels through the \nmiddle of the week. Rain chances today will be confined to Joshua \nTree National Park, with chances for wetting rainfall very low. \nMinRH's will generally be around 20-30% area wide through much of \nthis upcoming week with good overnight recoveries. Some elevated \nafternoon/early evening breeziness out of the northwest can be \nexpected this afternoon and evening with peak gusts of 20-30 mph. A \nmore typical diurnal wind pattern with some occasional afternoon \nupslope breeziness can be expected through much of this coming week.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Lojero\nLONG TERM...Berislavich/Lojero\nAVIATION...18\nFIRE WEATHER...Lojero/Berislavich\n",
